</div>

<main class="main">

  <div class="breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo;
    <a href="/species/finger-lime.html">Finger Lime</a> &rsaquo;
    Buying Guide
  </div>

  <!-- What is a finger lime -->
  <section class="section">
    <h2>What Is a Finger Lime?</h2>
    <p>
      The finger lime (<em>Microcitrus australasica</em>) is a native Australian rainforest citrus from the subtropical ranges
      of southeast Queensland and northern New South Wales. Its juice sacs are little round pearls that pop when you bite them, which is why chefs call it "citrus caviar".
    </p>
    <p>
      The fruit ranges from 4 to 12 cm long with skin colours spanning green, yellow, red, black, and pink depending on variety.
      The flesh inside comes in matching colour variations: green, yellow, pink, or red. All that colour is a big part of why collectors go after named varieties instead of seedlings.
    </p>
    <p>
      In the garden it's a dense, thorny shrub or small tree (3 to 6 metres, but easy to keep smaller with pruning). It'll handle shade but crops better in full sun. It suits subtropical, tropical and warm temperate climates, and more people are growing it in WA.
    </p>

    <div class="callout callout-green">
      <div class="callout-icon">🌿</div>
      <p><strong>Native plant:</strong> Finger lime is one of six native Australian citrus species and the most important one commercially. It's farmed for export and used in high-end restaurants around the world.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Varieties -->
  <section class="section">
    <h2>Finger Lime Varieties Available in Australia</h2>
    <p>
      With finger limes it's worth buying a named variety. Seedlings vary a lot in fruit quality, colour and yield, while a grafted or cutting-grown named variety gives you fruit that matches the parent. These are varieties that nurseries we track have had in stock.
    </p>

    <div class="variety-grid">
{% for v in varieties %}      <div class="variety-card">
        <div class="v-name">{{ v.name }}</div>
        <div class="v-desc">{{ v.desc }}</div>
        <span class="v-badge{% if not v.in_stock %} v-badge-red{% endif %}">{{ v.badge }}</span>
      </div>
{% endfor %}    </div>

    <p style="margin-top: 16px; font-size: 0.88rem; color: #6b7280;">
      Other varieties we've tracked: Emma, Gold Finger, Rainforest Pearl, Purple, Pink Ice, D'Emerald (Fat Black), Jali Red, Giant Jali Red,
      Gold Pearl, Jasper Jade, Little Ruby, BXL, Rosebank Red, Mt White, Russell River.
      See the <a href="/species/finger-lime.html">live tracker</a> for current availability.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Price guide -->
  <section class="section">
    <h2>Finger Lime Tree Price Guide</h2>
    <p>
      {% if min_price %}Right now finger limes go from {{ min_price }} to {{ max_price }} at the nurseries we track. {% endif %}The price mostly comes down to how it was propagated (cutting, grafted or seedling), the pot size and the variety.
    </p>

    <div class="price-tier">
      <div class="tier-price">Under $25</div>
      <div class="tier-body">
        <strong>Small cutting-grown plants (50mm to 90mm pots)</strong>
        <p>The cheapest way to get a named variety. They're young plants grown from cuttings, so they're true to name, but they'll take longer to get going and fruit than grafted trees. <em>{{ tiers[0] }}.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="price-tier">
      <div class="tier-price">$25 to $65</div>
      <div class="tier-body">
        <strong>Mid-size grafted or cutting-grown (140mm to 2L pots)</strong>
        <p>The most common price range. Grafted trees fruit faster than seedlings (2 to 3 years vs 5 to 7 from seed). <em>{{ tiers[1] }}.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="price-tier">
      <div class="tier-price">$65 to $100</div>
      <div class="tier-body">
        <strong>Larger grafted specimens (5L pots, advanced trees)</strong>
        <p>Well-established trees that may fruit in their first or second season. <em>{{ tiers[2] }}.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="price-tier">
      <div class="tier-price">$100+</div>
      <div class="tier-body">
        <strong>Large potted specimens and multi-grafted trees</strong>
        <p>Mature trees, so they should fruit quickly. Multi-grafted trees (finger lime plus another citrus on one tree) usually sit here too. <em>{{ tiers[3] }}.</em></p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h3>Current Prices by Nursery</h3>
{% if rows %}    <table class="price-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Nursery</th>
          <th>Price range</th>
          <th>In stock</th>
          <th>Notes</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
{% for r in rows %}        <tr>
          <td><a href="/nursery/{{ r.key }}.html" class="nursery-name">{{ r.name }}</a></td>
          <td><span class="price-range">{{ r.range }}</span></td>
          <td>{{ r.count }}</td>
          <td><span class="note{% if r.restricted %} restriction{% endif %}">{{ r.note }}</span></td>
        </tr>
{% endfor %}      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p style="font-size: 0.82rem; color: #9ca3af; margin-top: 8px;">In-stock finger limes only, from our check of each nursery's website on {{ today }}. Prices change, so check the nursery before you order. Every listing is on the <a href="/species/finger-lime.html">finger lime page</a>.</p>
{% else %}    <p>None of the nurseries we track have finger limes in stock right now. The <a href="/species/finger-lime.html">finger lime page</a> lists every variety, and you can set an alert on any of them.</p>
{% endif %}
  </section>

  <!-- Where to buy by state -->
  <section class="section">
    <h2>Where to Buy Finger Lime Trees in Australia (by State)</h2>
    <p>
      Finger lime counts as citrus, so citrus biosecurity rules apply in some states, mainly WA, NT and TAS. A lot of QLD nurseries only ship to QLD, NSW, VIC and ACT. Here's what you can get in each state.
    </p>

    <div class="state-grid">
{% for c in state_cards %}      <div class="state-card {{ 'ok' if c.ok else 'restricted' }}">
        <div class="state-name">{{ c.label }}</div>
        <div class="state-info">{{ c.info }}</div>
      </div>
{% endfor %}    </div>

    <h3>Buying Finger Limes in Western Australia</h3>
{% if wa %}    <p>
      WA has the most restrictions, but you've still got some options. These nurseries have finger limes in stock that they can get to you:
    </p>
    <ul>
{% for w in wa %}      <li><strong>{{ w.name }}</strong>: {{ w.line }}</li>
{% endfor %}    </ul>
{% else %}    <p>
      None of the nurseries with finger limes in stock right now can get one to WA. Set an alert on a variety from the <a href="/species/finger-lime.html">finger lime page</a>, choose WA as your state, and we'll email you when one you can buy turns up.
    </p>
{% endif %}
    <div class="callout callout-red">
      <div class="callout-icon">&#9888;</div>
      <p><strong>WA quarantine:</strong> Citrus plants coming into WA have to come from nurseries registered with DPIRD (WA's Department of Primary Industries and Regional Development). Only buy from a nursery that says it can ship citrus to WA.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Growing guide -->
  <section class="section">
    <h2>Growing Finger Limes in Australia</h2>

    <h3>Climate</h3>
    <p>
      Finger limes come from subtropical and warm temperate rainforest. They do best with warm to hot summers and mild winters. Once established they'll handle a light frost, but a heavy one will damage them. How they go around the country:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Subtropical Queensland, NSW north coast: ideal</li>
      <li>Sydney and coastal NSW: excellent, minimal frost protection needed</li>
      <li>Melbourne and VIC: grows well in sheltered positions, protect from heavy frost</li>
      <li>Perth (WA): well-suited, Mediterranean climate with warm dry summers</li>
      <li>Adelaide and SA: suitable in most areas</li>
      <li>Tropical QLD and NT: can grow but may struggle with waterlogging in wet season</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Soil and Position</h3>
    <p>
      Plant in free-draining soil, because finger lime really doesn't like wet feet. Raised beds, mounds, or sandy loam with some organic matter dug in are ideal. You'll get more fruit in full sun, though it copes with part shade. In pots, use a good citrus potting mix and make sure it drains well.
    </p>

    <h3>Grafted vs Cutting-Grown vs Seedling</h3>
    <p>
      Most nurseries sell either grafted or cutting-grown plants (and occasionally seeds or seedlings):
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Grafted trees</strong>: Fruit in 2 to 3 years. True-to-variety. Usually the best choice if you want fruit quickly. Slightly more expensive.</li>
      <li><strong>Cutting-grown plants</strong>: True-to-variety but slower to establish than grafted. Cheaper. Fruit Tree Lane specialises in cutting-grown plants.</li>
      <li><strong>Seedlings</strong>: May take 5 to 7 years to fruit. Fruit may not match the parent variety. Only worth it if you're experimenting or on a tight budget.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Pests and Disease</h3>
    <p>
      The main pests are scale (armoured and soft) and bronze orange bug, and you treat them the same way as on other citrus. In WA, citrus gall wasp is a biosecurity concern, so check any tree from interstate carefully and follow DPIRD's advice on getting rid of anything affected.
    </p>

    <h3>Harvesting</h3>
    <p>
      Finger limes typically fruit autumn through winter (March to August in most Australian climates). On most varieties the fruit doesn't change colour when it's ripe. Harvest when the fruit detaches easily with a gentle twist. Store in the fridge for
      up to two weeks, or freeze the juice vesicles for up to six months.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <div class="callout-icon">&#9201;</div>
      <p><strong>Be patient:</strong> finger lime is slow. Even grafted plants usually take 2 to 3 years to give you a decent crop. After that it's a long-lived, productive tree that's pretty tough once it's established.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Live tracker CTA -->
