  <!-- FAQ -->
  <section class="section" style="margin-top: 40px;">
    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">What is the cheapest way to buy a finger lime tree in Australia?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Small cutting-grown plants are usually the cheapest way to get a named variety. They're true to name, just small, so they need growing on.{% if cheapest %} Right now the cheapest we can see is {{ cheapest.title }} from {{ cheapest.nursery }} at {{ cheapest.price }}{% if cheapest.qld_only %} (QLD only){% endif %}.{% endif %}{% if cheapest_wa %} The cheapest that can get to WA is {{ cheapest_wa.title }} from {{ cheapest_wa.nursery }} at {{ cheapest_wa.price }}.{% endif %}</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">How long does a finger lime take to fruit?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Grafted trees typically produce their first crop in 2 to 3 years. Cutting-grown plants are slower, usually 3 to 4 years. Seedling-grown trees can take 5 to 7 years or more. If you want fruit sooner, buy a grafted tree.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Can I grow finger limes in a pot?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Yes. It does well in a big pot (50L or more). Use a good citrus potting mix, make sure it drains well, and feed it a slow-release citrus fertiliser. Dwarf varieties like Tom Thumb are especially good in pots.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Can I get finger limes delivered to Western Australia?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Yes, but you've got fewer options than in the eastern states.{% if wa_names %} Right now {{ wa_names }} {{ 'has' if wa|length == 1 else 'have' }} finger limes in stock that can get to WA.{% else %} Right now none of the nurseries with stock can send one to WA.{% endif %} Citrus from interstate has to come from a nursery registered with DPIRD.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">What is the difference between grafted and cutting-grown finger limes?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Both are true-to-variety (meaning the fruit will match the named variety). Grafted trees grow on a separate rootstock which can improve vigour and disease resistance. Cutting-grown trees grow on their own roots. In practice both do well, though grafted trees tend to fruit a bit sooner.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Why are some finger lime varieties restricted to QLD?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">In most cases it's the nursery's own rule, not state law. Some QLD nurseries have biosecurity accreditation that only covers certain states, and others may have quarantine concerns about particular citrus varieties. Check the nursery's current shipping policy before ordering.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Which finger lime variety is best for eating fresh?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Green Sapphire and Alstonville are popular for fresh eating and cooking, because the fruit is bigger and has a clean citrus flavour. People grow Red Champagne and Byron Sunrise for the colour. Judy's Everbearing is popular because it fruits over a longer season.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">How do I get alerted when a specific variety comes back in stock?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Set an alert on that variety from the finger lime species page and we'll email you when it's back.{% if digest_signup %} Or sign up below for an email with new arrivals and restocks across everything we track.{% endif %}</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

{% if digest_signup %}  <!-- Subscribe -->
  <div class="subscribe-box">
